<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">The workspace</span><h1>Product</h1><p>A practical productivity workspace that brings tasks, notes, projects and optional AI assistance into one focused place.</p></section><section class="section"><div class="section-inner">
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">01</span><h2>Tasks that stay actionable</h2><p>Create tasks with clear status, priority, due dates and descriptions. The interface is designed to make the next action easy to scan instead of burying it under unnecessary configuration.</p><p>Because tasks can connect to projects, users can keep individual actions connected to the outcome they support. That relationship makes the workspace useful for both personal planning and small-team coordination.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">02</span><h2>Notes that preserve context</h2><p>Capture research, decisions, ideas and meeting notes without leaving the workspace. Pin important notes so they remain easy to find when work becomes busy.</p><p>Notes are intentionally lightweight. The goal is to preserve useful context close to current work, not force every thought into a complex knowledge-management structure.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">03</span><h2>Projects that create structure</h2><p>Projects provide a higher-level view for related work. Dates, descriptions, status and milestones can describe the shape of an outcome while tasks provide the actions needed to reach it.</p><p>This structure supports simple planning without demanding a particular productivity methodology. A user can keep a project small or use it as the center of a larger piece of work.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">04</span><h2>Optional AI assistance</h2><p>PocketCat can connect to Amazon Bedrock through an administrator-configured API key or AWS credentials. Compatible models can be selected by model ID, and compatible custom endpoints can be configured.</p><p>AI is optional and usage can be controlled by account plan. Free accounts have a daily active-time allowance, while premium accounts can receive higher limits. Generated responses should always be reviewed before use.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">05</span><h2>Administration and control</h2><p>Administrators receive a dedicated control center for users, plans, database configuration, email, security, site settings and Bedrock. This keeps infrastructure-level controls away from normal users.</p><p>The first-run installer is designed to collect the required settings once and then lock itself. After installation, administration happens through the authenticated dashboard and hamburger menu.</p></article>
<article class="about-section"><span class="eyebrow">06</span><h2>Responsive by design</h2><p>The workspace is built to remain usable on phones and desktops. The navigation collapses into a hamburger menu on smaller screens, forms stack into a single column and public pages use wide content areas for comfortable reading.</p><p>The result is a product surface that can be used from a mobile browser without turning every screen into a miniature desktop layout.</p></article>
